1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,160
I'm Steve Kearns and I'm the District Advancement Chair with the Portola District in North Orange County, California.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:10,160
This edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,400
And now the old Scoutmaster,

4
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:26,560
Rolled Amundsen, the polar explorer, a man who had plenty of adventures, said that an adventure is merely a bit of bad planning.

5
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,000
Yeah, I guess that's true, isn't it?

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,040
Oh, let's not have too many adventures, okay.

7
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,800
Hey, this is podcast number 283..

8
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:12,960
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green. This is a holiday weekend and so this is a holiday podcast.

9
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:23,600
We'll get back to our usual format next week, but this week I have something to share with you that I think could potentially revolutionize the way that you approach your work as a scouter.

10
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:32,560
It really is that interesting and that important, and I think it's worthy of taking up the remainder of this podcast. so let's get started, shall we?

11
00:01:54,560 --> 00:02:03,600
I think what we need to look at is we need to look at learning as the product of educational self-organization.

12
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:13,600
If you allow the educational process to self-organize, then learning emerges. It's not about making learning happen, it's about letting it happen.

13
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:24,640
That was Dr Sugutramitra, and we're going to spend some time in this podcast kind of dissecting a TED talk that he gave a while back,

14
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:36,640
because I think it helps us get a handle on what I have talked about in the past, called the discovery process as the key to understanding the way that we work with scouts.

15
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:48,640
Now I'll have a link in the post that contains this podcast to the full TED talk that Dr Mitra gave, and I want to thank Craig Dixon for inspiring me to take another look at this.

16
00:02:48,640 --> 00:03:00,640
I listened to this TED talk quite a while back and I went back and listened again, and I think there's a lot for us to pick up from Dr Mitra's work that will inform our work as scouts.

17
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:08,640
One of the things that's really difficult to get is the process involved in scouting. As I said, I've called the discovery process before,

18
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:22,640
and the reason that it's difficult for us to get a handle on it is because it's something that is basically kind of unfamiliar and kind of at odds with the way that we educate children in our school system.

19
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:34,640
I think it's important to say that I know a lot of teachers and I really value their work, and I don't want anybody to take this as an indictment of the way that we educate children,

20
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:45,640
because as soon as you start talking about school systems and teachers and the educational system in the United States. we just enter into the territory of a lot of arguments, right?

21
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:53,640
So what I would like you to do is put all that aside and just listen to what Dr Mitra has to say about the way that we educate children.

22
00:03:53,640 --> 00:04:00,640
If you look at present day schooling the way it is, it's quite easy to figure out where it came from.

23
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:09,640
It came from about 300 years ago and it came from the last and the biggest of the empires on this planet.

24
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:25,640
Imagine trying to run the show, trying to run the entire planet without computers, without telephones, with data handwritten on pieces of paper and travelling by ships.

25
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:37,640
But the Victorians actually did it. What they did was amazing. They created a global computer made up of people.

26
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:45,640
It's still with us today. It's called the bureaucratic administrative machine.

27
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:51,640
In order to have that machine running, you need lots and lots of people.

28
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:58,640
They made another machine to produce those people: the school.

29
00:04:58,640 --> 00:05:08,640
The schools would produce the people who would then become parts of the bureaucratic administrative machine.

30
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:22,640
They must be identical to each other. They must be so identical that you could pick one up from New Zealand and ship them to Canada and he would be instantly functional.

31
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:31,640
The Victorians were great engineers. They engineered a system that was so robust that is still with us today,

32
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:41,640
Continuously producing identical people for a machine that no longer exists.

33
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:48,640
The empire is gone. So what are we doing with that design that produces these identical people,

34
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:55,640
And what are we going to do next, if we ever are going to do anything else with it?

35
00:05:55,640 --> 00:06:04,640
What we tend to do as scouters is to replicate that very highly engineered, very effective school approach to scouting,

36
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:11,640
And that means that we trade off the idea of self-organized learning or the discovery process,

37
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:16,640
Which is really at the heart of scouting and has been since it was founded.

38
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:22,640
So how do we understand more about the discovery process or self-organized learning?

39
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:28,640
Dr Mitra experimented with this, and I want to let him tell you the story of exactly what he did.

40
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:32,640
Well, I bumped into this whole thing completely by accident.

41
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:37,640
I used to teach people how to write computer programs in New Delhi 14 years ago,

42
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:41,640
And right next to where I used to work, there was a slum

43
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:46,640
And I used to think: how on earth are those kids ever going to learn to write computer programs,

44
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:49,640
Or should they not?

45
00:06:49,640 --> 00:06:53,640
I made a hole in the boundary wall of the slum next to my office

46
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:56,640
and I stuck a computer inside it just to see what would happen

47
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:59,640
If I gave a computer to children who never would have one,

48
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:02,640
Didn't know any English, didn't know what the internet was.

49
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:05,640
The children came running in- it was three feet off the ground and they said:

50
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:08,640
what is this? And I said, yeah, you know, I don't know.

51
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:13,640
So they said: why have you put it there?

52
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:14,640
I said just like that.

53
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:17,640
And they said: can we touch it? I said if you wish to,

54
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:20,640
And I went away.

55
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:23,640
About eight hours later we found them browsing

56
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:25,640
and teaching each other how to browse.

57
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:29,640
So I said: but that's impossible because you know. how is it possible?

58
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,640
They don't know anything.

59
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:34,640
My colleagues said, no, it's a simple solution.

60
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:38,640
One of your students must have been passing by showed them how to use the mouse.

61
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:40,640
So I said, yeah, that's possible.

62
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,640
So I repeated the experiment.

63
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:45,640
I went 300 miles out of Delhi into a really remote village

64
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:49,640
with the chances of you know a passing software development engineer.

65
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:53,640
It was very little.

66
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:56,640
I repeated the experiment there.

67
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:59,640
There was no place to stay, so I stuck my computer in. I went away,

68
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:02,640
came back after a couple of months, found kids playing games on it

69
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:03,640
And they saw me.

70
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:10,640
They said we want a faster processor and a better mouse.

71
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:13,640
So I said: how on earth do you know all this?

72
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:17,640
And they said something very interesting to me in an irritated voice.

73
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:20,640
They said: you've given us a machine that works only in English,

74
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:26,640
So we had to teach ourselves English in order to use it.

75
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:30,640
That's the first time as a teacher that I heard the word

76
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:33,640
teach ourselves so casually.

77
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:35,640
So this is a fascinating idea.

78
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:40,640
Dr Mitra takes a computer, puts it in a hole in the wall in a slum in New Delhi

79
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:46,640
and lets children just work with the computer and they begin to learn things.

80
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,640
They organize their own learning

81
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:50,640
And the outcome doesn't seem very likely, does it?

82
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:55,640
Who would expect children to be able to do anything with something so complex

83
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:59,640
and so complicated? but they began to understand what it was

84
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:02,640
and began to understand how to use it.

85
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:06,640
And this is what I've talked about in the past as the discovery process in scouting:

86
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,640
Not making learning happen, but providing the opportunities,

87
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:13,640
creating the environment where this kind of learning happens.

88
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:17,640
Now we're not teaching our scouts the practicalities of how to use a computer.

89
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:22,640
Skills that we use and the activities that we use are very, very different from that.

90
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:25,640
But the basics are the same.

91
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:29,640
What it is- that, given the opportunity, given the right environment,

92
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:32,640
they will organize the way that they're going to learn

93
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,640
and they'll really get something out of it.

94
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:38,640
And we know that the point of this is not the skills themselves.

95
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:43,640
The skills are something that we use to develop their character

96
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:48,640
and develop the skills of learning, how to learn.

97
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,640
So let's go back to Dr Mitra for a moment,

98
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:54,640
because his first experiment was right outside his office.

99
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:57,640
The idea was that, oh, perhaps somebody with more knowledge

100
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:59,640
could have come along and taught the children.

101
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:04,640
So he moved that out to this more remote location and had the same result.

102
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:08,640
And then he decides this

103
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:12,640
People said: well, how far will it go? Where does it stop?

104
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:18,640
I decided I would destroy my own argument by creating an absurd proposition.

105
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:22,640
I made a hypothesis, a ridiculous hypothesis.

106
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:24,640
Tamil is a South Indian language

107
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:27,640
And I said: can Tamil-speaking children in a South Indian village

108
00:10:27,640 --> 00:10:32,640
learn the biotechnology of DNA replication in English from a street-side computer?

109
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:35,640
And I said I'll measure them, They'll get a zero.

110
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:38,640
I'll leave it for a couple of months, I'll go back.

111
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:40,640
They'll get another zero.

112
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:44,640
I'll go back to the lab and say: we need teachers.

113
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:48,640
I found a village- It was called Kalikukpam, in Southern India.

114
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:51,640
I put in a whole lot of computers there,

115
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:55,640
Downloaded all kinds of stuff from the internet about DNA replication,

116
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:58,640
most of which I didn't understand.

117
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:01,640
The children came rushing and said: what's all this?

118
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:05,640
So I said it's very topical, very important.

119
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:07,640
It's all in English.

120
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:10,640
So they said: how can we understand such big English words

121
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:12,640
and diagrams and chemistry?

122
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:15,640
So by then I had developed a new pedagogical method,

123
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:18,640
So I applied that. I said I haven't the foggiest idea

124
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:22,640
And anyway, I'm going away.

125
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:30,640
So I left them for a couple of months.

126
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:33,640
They'd got a zero. I gave them a test.

127
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:36,640
I came back after two months and the children trooped in

128
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:38,640
and said: we've understood nothing.

129
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,640
So what did I expect?

130
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:45,640
So I said: okay, but how long did it take you before you decided

131
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:47,640
that you can't understand anything?

132
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,640
So they said: we haven't given up.

133
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:51,640
We look at it every single day.

134
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:54,640
So I said what Don't understand these screens?

135
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:56,640
and you keep staring at it for two months? what for?

136
00:11:56,640 --> 00:11:59,640
So a little girl who you'll see just now.

137
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:02,640
she raised her hand and she says to me in broken Tamil and English:

138
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:05,640
she said: well, apart from the fact that improper replication

139
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:07,640
of the DNA molecule causes disease.

140
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:09,640
we haven't understood anything else.

141
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:19,640
So I tested them.

142
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:21,640
I got an educational impossibility:

143
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:25,640
zero to 30% in two months, in the tropical heat,

144
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:27,640
with their computer under the tree in a language

145
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:29,640
that they didn't know, doing something

146
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:31,640
that's a decade ahead of their time-

147
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:33,640
Absurd.

148
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:37,640
But I had to follow the Victorian norm.

149
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:40,640
30% is a fail.

150
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:42,640
How do I get them to pass?

151
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:44,640
I have to get them 20 more marks.

152
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:46,640
I couldn't find a teacher.

153
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:48,640
What I did find was a friend that they had

154
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:51,640
A 22-year-old girl who was an accountant

155
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:53,640
and she played with them all the time.

156
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:55,640
So I asked this girl: can you help them?

157
00:12:55,640 --> 00:12:58,640
So she says absolutely not.

158
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:00,640
I didn't have science in school.

159
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:03,640
I have no idea what they're doing under the tree all day long.

160
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,640
I can't help you.

161
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:08,640
I'm really struck with the parallel

162
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:10,640
that this story has with the way

163
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,640
a lot of us become scouts.

164
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,640
In this very remote village,

165
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:16,640
Dr Mitra is doing another experiment

166
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:19,640
and he decides that he needs somebody as a catalyst.

167
00:13:19,640 --> 00:13:21,640
He approaches this young woman

168
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:25,640
and she says: I have no skills at all.

169
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:27,640
I don't know what you're talking about.

170
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,640
I have no idea what they're doing under that tree all day.

171
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:33,640
And does that sound familiar to you?

172
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:36,640
Perhaps somebody approached you about becoming a scouter

173
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:38,640
and you said: I really doubt

174
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:40,640
that I would be very effective at doing this.

175
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:42,640
I have not a lot of experience working with children,

176
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:44,640
or I don't really know how to teach

177
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:47,640
and all the various excuses that we come up with.

178
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,640
But Dr Mitra's advice to this young lady

179
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:54,640
is one that we should really listen to very, very carefully,

180
00:13:54,640 --> 00:13:58,640
because he explains how to facilitate

181
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:01,640
this self-organized learning process

182
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:04,640
very succinctly and very creatively.

183
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:06,640
I said I'll tell you what.

184
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:08,640
use the method of the grandmother.

185
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,640
So she says: what's that?

186
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:11,640
I said: stand behind them.

187
00:14:11,640 --> 00:14:13,640
Whenever they do anything, you just say:

188
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:15,640
well, wow, I mean, how did you do that?

189
00:14:15,640 --> 00:14:17,640
What's the next page?

190
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:19,640
Gosh, when I was your age, I could have never done that.

191
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:21,640
I mean, you know what grannies do.

192
00:14:21,640 --> 00:14:24,640
So she did that. for two more months.

193
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:26,640
The scores jumped to 50%.

194
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:28,640
Encouragement seems to be the key.

195
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:31,640
If you look at all of the experiments that I did,

196
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:37,640
it was simply saying wow, saluting learning.

197
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:41,640
Let's take a closer look at Dr Mitra's granny method.

198
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:45,640
Be present, be encouraging, ask questions

199
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:49,640
and say wow and salute the results,

200
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:51,640
Acknowledge the results.

201
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:53,640
I think that bears repeating.

202
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:58,640
Be present, be encouraging, ask questions,

203
00:14:58,640 --> 00:15:04,640
say wow and salute the learning that results.

204
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:07,640
The granny method is really radically different

205
00:15:07,640 --> 00:15:09,640
than what we're familiar with,

206
00:15:09,640 --> 00:15:12,640
which is the school method, which is: be the expert,

207
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:15,640
teach you know, lay everything out,

208
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:19,640
then help them practice everything and then test it.

209
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:21,640
Now, that method we know that's effective

210
00:15:21,640 --> 00:15:24,640
and we know that's the way that our children learn

211
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:26,640
most things that they learn.

212
00:15:26,640 --> 00:15:29,640
but that doesn't mean we should replicate

213
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:32,640
that kind of lecturing, practicing,

214
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:35,640
testing method in scouting,

215
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:38,640
because what we're trying to create

216
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:40,640
is something quite different than somebody

217
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:43,640
who can effectively pass a test.

218
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:47,640
We're trying to create a person of good character

219
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:50,640
with the confidence to learn for themselves.

220
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:55,640
Dr Mitra discusses something that helps inform that method.

221
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:57,640
There is evidence from neuroscience,

222
00:15:57,640 --> 00:15:59,640
The reptilian part of our brain

223
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:01,640
which sits in the center of our brain.

224
00:16:01,640 --> 00:16:04,640
when it's threatened it shuts down everything else.

225
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:06,640
It shuts down the prefrontal cortex,

226
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:08,640
the parts which learn.

227
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:10,640
It shuts all of that down.

228
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:14,640
Punishment and examinations are seen as threats.

229
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,640
We take our children, we make them shut their brains down

230
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:21,640
and then we say perform.

231
00:16:21,640 --> 00:16:24,640
Why did they create a system like that?

232
00:16:24,640 --> 00:16:26,640
Because it was needed.

233
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:29,640
There was an age, in the Age of Empires,

234
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:33,640
when you needed those people who can survive under threat.

235
00:16:33,640 --> 00:16:36,640
When you're standing in a trench all alone,

236
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:38,640
if you could have survived, you're okay,

237
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:40,640
You passed.

238
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:42,640
If you didn't, you failed.

239
00:16:42,640 --> 00:16:45,640
But the Age of Empires is gone.

240
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:47,640
So what's happening here?

241
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:49,640
I think what we need to look at

242
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:51,640
is we need to look at learning

243
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:56,640
as the product of educational self-organization.

244
00:16:56,640 --> 00:16:59,640
If you allow the educational process to self-organize,

245
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:01,640
then learning emerges.

246
00:17:01,640 --> 00:17:04,639
It's not about making learning happen,

247
00:17:04,639 --> 00:17:06,639
It's about letting it happen.

248
00:17:06,639 --> 00:17:09,639
The teacher sets the process in motion

249
00:17:09,639 --> 00:17:12,639
and then she stands back in awe

250
00:17:12,639 --> 00:17:14,639
and watches as learning happens.

251
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:17,640
I think that's what all this is pointing at.

252
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:19,640
So let's make sure we stay focused

253
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:21,640
on the way that we can apply this

254
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:23,640
in our work as scouters.

255
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:26,640
Understanding that the methods that we learned

256
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:30,640
in school and that our children still learn in school

257
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:33,640
is not the approach that we're looking at in scouting. right,

258
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:35,640
We're looking at the granny method

259
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:37,640
and the discovery process,

260
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,640
And that's the way scouting was built.

261
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:42,640
It was built to be experiential

262
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:47,640
and to have these self-organizing learning experiences.

263
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:49,640
I've talked about this on this theoretical level,

264
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:51,640
which I know can be a little tiring,

265
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:54,640
So let's look at it on a very practical level.

266
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:57,640
Our scouts organize themselves into patrols

267
00:17:57,640 --> 00:17:59,640
and the patrols are challenged

268
00:17:59,640 --> 00:18:02,640
to carry out experiential activities

269
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:04,640
that inspire them to learn,

270
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:06,640
Because patrols do what scouts do

271
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:09,640
Scouts go camping? and to get camping,

272
00:18:09,640 --> 00:18:11,640
you have to learn the skills of building a fire

273
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,640
and cooking over a fire and setting up tents

274
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:15,640
and choosing a campsite.

275
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,640
You have to learn how to purify water

276
00:18:17,640 --> 00:18:19,640
and why you have to purify water,

277
00:18:19,640 --> 00:18:21,640
And to be comfortable in doing that.

278
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:24,640
you need to understand the natural world around you

279
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:27,640
and how to build the foundations of working together

280
00:18:27,640 --> 00:18:29,640
as human beings. right,

281
00:18:29,640 --> 00:18:31,640
You have to rebuild your little outpost

282
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:33,640
of society in the woods

283
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,640
And to do that you have to know experientially

284
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:39,640
a great number of very practical things.

285
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,640
But let's remember that the practical knowledge

286
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:46,640
is all subservient to the main aim

287
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:49,640
of developing character and confidence

288
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:52,640
and the ability to learn and the skills of leadership

289
00:18:52,640 --> 00:18:54,640
and the skills of responsibility.

290
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:56,640
That's what we're aiming at.

291
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:58,640
Dr Mitra closes his TED Talk

292
00:18:58,640 --> 00:19:02,640
by talking about a curriculum of questions,

293
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,640
And I think it's an important thing

294
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:06,640
for us to think about.

295
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:08,640
I think we need a curriculum of big questions.

296
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:10,640
There was a time when

297
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:13,640
Stone Age men and women used to sit and look up at the sky

298
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,640
and say: what are those twinkling lights?

299
00:19:15,640 --> 00:19:18,640
But we've lost sight of that wondrous question.

300
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:20,640
This is done by children

301
00:19:20,640 --> 00:19:23,640
without the help of any teacher.

302
00:19:23,640 --> 00:19:26,640
The teacher only raises the question

303
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:29,640
and then stands back and admires the answer.

304
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:32,640
My wish is to help design a future of learning

305
00:19:32,640 --> 00:19:34,640
by supporting children all over the world

306
00:19:34,640 --> 00:19:37,640
to tap into their wonder and their ability to work together.

307
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:39,640
It'll be a school

308
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:42,640
where children go on these intellectual adventures

309
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:44,640
driven by the big questions,

310
00:19:44,640 --> 00:19:46,640
with their mediators put in.

311
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:49,640
You can do self-organized learning environments

312
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:51,640
at home, in the school,

313
00:19:51,640 --> 00:19:54,640
outside of school, in clubs.

314
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:56,640
It's very easy to do.

315
00:19:56,640 --> 00:19:58,640
There's a great document produced by TED

316
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:00,640
which tells you how to do it.

317
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:02,640
If you would, please, please, do it.

318
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:04,640
That's my wish.

319
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:07,640
I'll link both to the complete TED talk

320
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:11,640
and the document that Dr Mitra referred to here,

321
00:20:11,640 --> 00:20:13,640
And there's a lot of valuable information

322
00:20:13,640 --> 00:20:16,640
that you can get out of that document as a scouter.

323
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:18,640
But let's keep it very, very simple.

324
00:20:18,640 --> 00:20:21,640
Scouting is not based on

325
00:20:21,640 --> 00:20:24,640
the traditional structure of schools,

326
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:26,640
And after he founded the scouting movement,

327
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:28,640
Baden Powell spent a lot of time

328
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:30,640
pointing out that very thing

329
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:33,640
and discouraging people from using school methods

330
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:35,640
to work with scouts.

331
00:20:35,640 --> 00:20:38,640
Dr Mitra's self-organized learning environments,

332
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:40,640
or learning experiences,

333
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:43,640
is really what Baden Powell was talking about,

334
00:20:43,640 --> 00:20:46,640
in the way that he envisioned scouting

335
00:20:46,640 --> 00:20:49,640
working in that very experiential manner.

336
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:52,640
What I hope is: I hope this really gets you thinking

337
00:20:52,640 --> 00:20:54,640
and challenges you to look at

338
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:56,640
the way that you work with scouts

339
00:20:56,640 --> 00:20:58,640
and to think about that granny method,

340
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:00,640
which I think is really, really wonderful.

341
00:21:00,640 --> 00:21:02,640
Be present, be encouraging,

342
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:04,640
take lots of questions

343
00:21:04,640 --> 00:21:07,640
and recognize how wonderful the answers are

344
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:08,640
that they come up with.

345
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:11,640
I believe this to be the most effective way

346
00:21:11,640 --> 00:21:13,640
of getting to our goal

347
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:15,640
of achieving the aim

348
00:21:15,640 --> 00:21:17,640
that we have as scouts,

349
00:21:17,640 --> 00:21:19,640
which is the development of that resilient,

350
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:21,640
self-confident character

351
00:21:21,640 --> 00:21:23,640
that is able to take on responsibilities

352
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:25,640
and leadership

353
00:21:25,640 --> 00:21:27,640
and that contributes to every community

354
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:29,640
that our scouts are involved in.

355
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:32,640
So you can go and tell all your fellow scouts

356
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:35,640
that I said you have to become a granny, okay,

357
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:38,640
But there's a lot of sense in it

358
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:40,640
when you take a look at it.

359
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:42,640
Once again, be present, be encouraging,

360
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:44,640
ask lots of questions

361
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:47,640
and salute the learning that happens

